--TEST--
A Core\Ast\Node's line, column and offset are its production's first character, named at both ends of the file — `rule:core-classes/ast-is-inert`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The *bound* shape, over the two positions a reader can count off the source
// by hand: the first production in the file and the last. A member reporting a
// node's *end*, or the enclosing statement's start, prints plausibly against
// either one alone — the first node of a file shares its position with the
// statement holding it, and the last is the only place an end-of-production
// reading is unmistakable.
//
// The source is one literal so the numbers below are read off it directly:
// `<?nvs` and its newline are six bytes, so line 2 starts at offset 6.
var $src = "<?nvs\necho 1 + 2;\nif (1) { echo 3; }\n";
var $tree = Core\Ast::parse($src);

// The root is the file, which starts where its first statement does rather
// than at the `<?nvs` the parser consumed to reach it.
echo $tree->kind(), " ", $tree->line(), ":", $tree->column(), " @", $tree->offset(), "\n";

// One line each for the whole walk, in source order: a nested production's
// position is its own first character and never its parent's.
foreach ($tree->nodes() as Core\Ast\Node $each) {
    echo $each->kind(), " ", $each->line(), ":", $each->column(), " @", $each->offset(), "\n";
}

// The two ends as a pair, which is what the printed block alone does not
// assert: the root's position is its first child's, and the last node of the
// walk starts after every node before it, on a later line.
var $walk = $tree->nodes();
var $first = $walk[0];
var $last = $walk[Core\Arr::count($walk) - 1];
echo $tree->offset() == $first->offset() ? "the file starts where its first statement does" : "the file starts elsewhere", "\n";
echo $last->offset() > $first->offset() ? "the last node starts after the first" : "the walk is out of order", "\n";
echo $last->line() > $first->line() ? "and on a later line" : "and on the same line", "\n";
--EXPECT--
File 2:1 @6
Echo 2:1 @6
Binary 2:6 @11
Int 2:6 @11
Int 2:10 @15
If 3:1 @18
Int 3:5 @22
Block 3:8 @25
Echo 3:10 @27
Int 3:15 @32
the file starts where its first statement does
the last node starts after the first
and on a later line
